# Test source IO and normalization attribution

`probe.rs` calls the actual runtime IO/view/expected/admission functions on three
committed Rust source inputs, without replacing any guard. Three first historical
input-preparation spans are retained separately. The measured historical cache is
explicitly prepared; current files are still freshly read by each IO invocation.
This is the current runtime-IO/gate path, not every historical normalization chain.

The fixed 144 samples cover six alternating operation-order blocks. Independent
syntax, expected-token and actual-token controls are not additive phases of an
end-to-end call. Windows parent CPU excludes Git children and can be quantized;
observer measurements are retained without subtraction. No allocator interposes
on this timing image. Ordinary tests do not measure without the explicit input.

`capture.py` checks exact committed inputs, compiled source and binary identity,
uses an exclusive first-output directory and retains refusals before owned-tree
cleanup. No retry, package replay, product or server action is part of this tool.
`protocol.toml` fixes the attribution scope; `candidate-protocol.toml` separately
preregisters one potential streaming-display candidate, not its acceptance.

That prototype is not admitted: its sole 72-sample cohort has mixed within-process
wall observations and no allocation/independent CPU proof. Its hookup, source and
driver are removed from the active tree but recoverable from commit `2568f111`
and the [evidence packet and report](../../docs/testing/performance/0.74/normalization-cost-result-20261025.md).
`candidate-outcome.toml` records the withdrawal. The installed source gate never
uses the candidate; seven active tests and the attribution remain.
